  "exit_criteria": [
    "state the three pillars of the rational-agent model (rational beliefs, consistent preferences over risk and time, self-interest) and say which pillar a given anomaly violates",
    "recognize anchoring, availability, representativeness (conjunction fallacy, base-rate neglect), overconfidence, confirmation bias and framing in an unfamiliar scenario",
    "compute the expected value of a gamble and contrast it with a prospect-theory valuation using a reference point and a loss-aversion coefficient",
    "explain the Allais and Ellsberg paradoxes, the endowment effect, the fourfold pattern of risk attitudes and mental accounting, with one real-world example each",
    "use the beta-delta model to predict a preference reversal, and tell naive from sophisticated present-biased agents",
    "describe the ultimatum, dictator, trust and public-goods games, and use Fehr-Schmidt inequity aversion to find a minimum acceptable offer",
    "explain how incentives can crowd out intrinsic motivation, citing the day-care fine study",
    "design a nudge for a stated problem, then name a sludge risk and an ethical objection to it",
    "judge a behavioral finding's credibility: lab versus field, sample size, effect size, publication bias and replication status",
    "explain the disposition effect, limits to arbitrage and how firms profit from shrouded fees and naive consumers",
    "diagnose a real-world decision or market using several behavioral concepts together"
  ],
